@startuml
!include ../../../tools/diagrams/kcna-theme.puml
title From nothing to a working kubeconfig — every command, in order

rectangle "STAGE A · on YOUR machine — Kubernetes is not involved yet" <<group>> as a {
  rectangle "1 · a private key\n<size:11>openssl genrsa -out batman.key 4096</size>\n<size:11>NEVER leaves your machine and is never sent anywhere</size>" <<plain>> as a1
  rectangle "2 · a Certificate Signing Request, signed by that key\n<size:11>openssl req -new -key batman.key -out batman.csr \\</size>\n<size:11>  -subj “/CN=batman/O=cluster-superheroes” -sha256</size>\n\n<size:11>CN = the USERNAME you are asking for · O = the GROUP you are asking for</size>\n<size:11>A request, not a grant. Anyone can ask for anything.</size>" <<accent>> as a2
  a1 -down-> a2
}

rectangle "STAGE B · hand it to Kubernetes" <<group>> as b {
  rectangle "3 · base64 the CSR and build the API object\n<size:11>CSR_DATA=$(base64 batman.csr | tr -d '\\n')      ← tr strips the line wraps</size>\n<size:11>CSR_USER=batman</size>\n\n<size:11>apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1 · kind: CertificateSigningRequest</size>\n<size:11>metadata.name: ${CSR_USER}   ← the OBJECT name, NOT the username</size>\n<size:11>spec.request: ${CSR_DATA}</size>\n<size:11>spec.signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</size>\n<size:11>spec.usages: [client auth]    ← required by that signer</size>" <<accent>> as b1
  rectangle "4 · submit it\n<size:11>kubectl apply -f batman-csr-request.yaml</size>\n<size:11>kubectl get csr   →  CONDITION: Pending · REQUESTOR: system:admin</size>" <<plain>> as b2
  b1 -down-> b2
}

rectangle "STAGE C · an ADMIN approves — the CA signs" <<groupBad>> as c {
  rectangle "5 · approval\n<size:11>kubectl certificate approve batman</size>\n<size:11>kubectl get csr batman   →  CONDITION: Approved,Issued</size>\n\n<size:11>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client is NEVER auto-approved — a human with RBAC</size>\n<size:11>permission on certificatesigningrequests/approval must act. THIS is the control.</size>\n<size:11>And the CertificateSubjectRestriction admission plugin blocks O=system:masters outright.</size>" <<bad>> as c1
}

rectangle "STAGE D · collect the signed certificate" <<group>> as d {
  rectangle "6 · extract it\n<size:11>kubectl get csr batman -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > batman.crt</size>\n<size:11>openssl x509 -in batman.crt -noout -subject -issuer -dates   ← check what you actually got</size>" <<plain>> as d1
}

rectangle "STAGE E · assemble the kubeconfig" <<groupGood>> as e {
  rectangle "7 · start from a copy and strip the old identity — KEEP the cluster block\n<size:11>cp ~/.kube/config batman.config</size>\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl config unset users.default</size>\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl config delete-context default</size>\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl config unset current-context</size>\n\n<size:11>→ users: null · contexts: null · clusters: KEPT (the server URL and the CA public cert)</size>\n<size:11>The cluster block is the half of chapter 05-02 that is not a secret — reuse it.</size>" <<good>> as e1
  rectangle "8 · add the new credentials and a context\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl config set-credentials batman \\</size>\n<size:11>  ~-~-client-certificate=batman.crt ~-~-client-key=batman.key ~-~-embed-certs=true</size>\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl config set-context batman ~-~-cluster=default ~-~-user=batman</size>\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl config use-context batman</size>" <<good>> as e2
  rectangle "9 · use it\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl get nodes        → works</size>\n<size:11>KUBECONFIG=batman.config kubectl run nginx ~-~-image=nginx   → pod/nginx created</size>\n\n<size:11>...because O=cluster-superheroes matches the ClusterRoleBinding from chapter 05-03.</size>\n<size:11>Change the O and the same pipeline produces an identity that can do nothing.</size>" <<good>> as e3
  e1 -down-> e2
  e2 -down-> e3
}

a -down-> b
b -down-> c
c -down-> d
d -down-> e

caption <i>Nine commands, and not one of them created a user object — because none exists to create. The only thing Kubernetes stored was a CertificateSigningRequest, and even that is deleted afterwards.</i>\n<i>What persists is a signed certificate on a laptop whose Subject the cluster will believe. spurin/kubeconfig-creator wraps exactly these steps into one script with five stages.</i>
@enduml
